# Lab 19 — Legend Panel

## Objective

Can three investor skills analyze the same 10-K financial data consistently while reaching different investment conclusions?

I extended my Lab 18 Buffett analysis to include Cathie Wood and Michael Burry. All three investors use the same SEC EDGAR data, but each applies different financial checks before producing a qualitative recommendation.

## Known-Answer Validation — Costco (COST)

I tested the three-investor panel against Costco's known answers.

| Investor | Check | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Buffett | ROE ≥ 15% every year | Pass |
| Buffett | Positive owner earnings every year | Pass |
| Buffett | Owner earnings increased | Pass |
| Cathie Wood | Revenue CAGR ≥ 15% | Fail (7.51%) |
| Cathie Wood | Latest R&D/revenue ≥ 5% | Not known |
| Michael Burry | Operating cash flow ≥ net income every year | Pass |
| Michael Burry | Latest liabilities/equity ≤ 2 | Pass (1.49×) |

The Costco panel matched the expected results. R&D remained "not found" rather than being replaced with an estimate.

## Cathie Wood Threshold Probe

I lowered Cathie Wood's revenue-growth threshold from 15% to 5%. Costco's growth check changed from Fail to Pass, while its R&D check remained Not known.

**Threshold decision:** I chose to retain the original 15% threshold because it better reflects the high-growth philosophy being modeled. Lowering the threshold simply to make Costco pass would weaken the purpose of the screen.

## My Company — Apple (AAPL)

I selected Apple from QQQ, one of the ETFs in my Project 2 investment universe.

| Investor | Check | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Buffett | ROE ≥ 15% every year | Pass |
| Buffett | Positive owner earnings every year | Pass |
| Buffett | Owner earnings increased | Pass |
| Cathie Wood | Revenue CAGR ≥ 15% | Fail (4.97%) |
| Cathie Wood | Latest R&D/revenue ≥ 5% | Pass (9.56%) |
| Michael Burry | Operating cash flow ≥ net income every year | Fail |
| Michael Burry | Latest liabilities/equity ≤ 2 | Fail (2.61×) |

### Where the Panel Agreed and Split

Buffett's framework favored Apple because it passed all three profitability and owner-earnings checks. Cathie Wood recognized its R&D investment but rejected its revenue-growth rate, while Burry flagged both cash conversion and leverage concerns.

The panel demonstrated that identical financial data can support different conclusions depending on which risks and financial characteristics each investor prioritizes.

### Bull Case

Apple's strong returns on equity and consistently positive owner earnings suggest that it can generate cash after capital spending. Its R&D intensity also passes Cathie Wood's innovation threshold, supporting the argument that Apple continues investing in future products.

### Bear Case

Apple's 4.97% revenue CAGR falls short of the high-growth threshold, suggesting slower expansion than Cathie Wood's framework requires. It also fails both of Burry's checks, including a 2.61× liabilities-to-equity ratio, raising concerns that warrant further review.

